{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-Psoriasis-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filename": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-Psoriasis-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filepath": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-Psoriasis-guidelines-2025.pdf", "page": 2, "title": "FSH Dermatology Psoriasis Guidelines", "year": "2025", "producer": "Adobe PDF Library 21.5.90", "creator": "Acrobat PDFMaker 21 for Word", "creationdate": "2025-05-20T10:40:48+08:00", "author": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology", "company": "WA Health", "contenttypeid": "0x010100D20558782E22444F8E6476743C0A7A47", "keywords": "Fiona Stanley Hospital, FSH, dermatology, pre-referral guidelines, psoriasis", "moddate": "2025-05-20T10:40:49+08:00", "sourcemodified": "D:20250520024043", "subject": "Information on referring, treament and introduction to Psoriasis", "_dlc_dociditemguid": "400bd398-572f-4117-a0d8-763933fbd180", "total_pages": 5, "page_label": "3"}, "page_content": "Dermatology pre-referral guidelines \u2013 Psoriasis May2025 Page 3 of 5 \nGeneral measures \n\u2022 Minimise trauma to the skin \u2013 up to 75% of patients with psoriasis will develop psoriasis at \nthe site of skin injury (known as the Koebner phenomenon). \n\u2022 Gentle skin care measures will optimise barrier function of the skin and minimise scaling \nand irritation, i.e. avoid soap, use a soap-free wash, apply an emollient daily.  \n\u2022 Medicated shampoos can be used daily when scalp psoriasis is flaring (as per scalp \nseborrhoeic dermatitis), and then tapered to minimal effective dose (i.e. 1-2 times weekly) \nwhen controlled. They should be left in contact with the scalp for 3-5 minutes before rinsing \noff. These include: Nizoral Shampoo\u00ae, Dercos Shampoo\u00ae, T-Gel Shampoo\u00ae, Sebitar \nScalp Treatment\u00ae. \n\u2022 Gentle sun exposure can be helpful \u2013 up to 20 minutes sun on exposed skin in non-peak \nUVR times (i.e. UV index <3), up to three times per week.  \n\u2022 Obtain and maintain a healthy body weight \u2013 psoriasis is associated with the metabolic \nsyndrome. \n\u2022 Minimise alcohol intake and smoking.  \n\u2022 Minimise stress \u2013 up to 50% of patients with psoriasis will have worsening of their psoriasis \nwith worry/stress.", "type": "Document"}}